  "allocation_scope": "Raw MoonBit allocations in generated C and runtime, including headers. Whole-process counts divided by all callbacks; setup/fixture/benchmark overhead remains. Instrumented speed is excluded. RSS is uninstrumented whole-process kernel peak, not logical live memory.",
  "verdict": "neutral with small observed valid-input gains",
  "reason": "Valid scalar WAST lowering costs 72.954 -> 71.320 us for 256 signed-small constants (-2.24%); signed minimum 85.392 -> 83.038 us (-2.76%); unsigned cases -1.98% and -2.43%. The unchanged no-constant control is +0.65%; AA/BB drift reaches 2.07% for valid cases. Treat these small gains conservatively as neutral. Valid-input raw allocations and bytes are unchanged within amortized setup noise; native RSS is within same-build variation. Both invalid bounds now reject with the expected range error; their shorter rejection paths are diagnostic only. All 140 samples started at or below 65 C (maximum 64.875 C), peak 77.75 C; none paused or discarded.",
  "limits": "Whole-module parser/lowerer workload; no isolated parser-only speed claim. Counts include amortized fixture and runner overhead; RSS is process peak rather than live IR memory.",
